ld pay $$6.45$ for $3$ pounds of candy."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495046 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d550bb4f-72a6-5b74-b050-8fdc2af6b23f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Total Price from a Unit Price"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c222935-d93b-5e78-933f-9c2c8c1c2e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.00$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494636} {:answer/id #uuid "8c71cfd9-e460-5db1-9ac4-078942b91db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494637} {:answer/id #uuid "9547d47f-bad6-5c7f-a5e6-e86a971622b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1.50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494661} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ef5cff-dd64-525d-b67c-1f30b017df18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494688} {:answer/id #uuid "d5570aa3-ff70-5609-927c-8e74616b5285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3.00$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c71cfd9-e460-5db1-9ac4-078942b91db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494637} :answer-field/id #uuid "b289910e-c751-510e-8984-cc5dcbe2d43f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a794af8-7414-51a7-a6c7-994b60009b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-355003" :question/problem "Jenny is going to the craft store to buy some supplies. How much would it cost her to buy $\\frac{1}{2}$ of a yard of gold ribbon?\n\n| Item | Price |\n| --- | --- |\n| Gold Ribbon | $$5/\\text{yd}$ |\n| Silver Ribbon | $$4.5/\\text{yd}$ |" :question/worked-solution "The unit price of gold ribbon is $$5/\\text{yd}.$ To find the cost of  $\\frac{1}{2}$ of a yard of gold ribbon, we multiply: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\times \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{5}{1}\\times \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 1}{1\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =5÷2 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it would cost Jenny $$2.50$ to buy $\\frac{1}{2}$ of a yard of gold ribbon."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495042 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04d9118c-9cc2-5bb5-a7c4-ce43be06a65d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Best Deal by Computing Unit Prices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3044ea68-e73b-56dd-9a11-c82e1d203a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494414} {:answer/id #uuid "92f59a17-f5a0-5c24-bdb3-c57c8f1f241f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494652} {:answer/id #uuid "a67c690a-a1dc-5445-9741-577c1604442a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494700} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7ad806-1c43-582c-b01a-34db879f747e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494720} {:answer/id #uuid "e28759b2-e789-5d7c-847c-325d3f81b586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92f59a17-f5a0-5c24-bdb3-c57c8f1f241f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494652} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1856c59-881f-5703-8688-905238b1066e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60c429d6-78bf-52f4-a402-0b1e67fa2508" :question/math-academy-id "q-355005" :question/problem "David needs to decide what bag of flour to buy from three different types available at the grocery store:\n\n- Bag $A$ weighs $5$ kilograms, and costs $$7.50\\,.$\n\n- Bag $B$ weighs $2$ kilograms, and costs $$3.20\\,.$\n\n- Bag $C$ weighs $1$ kilogram, and costs $$1.70\\,.$\n\nWhich bag of flour is the best deal?" :question/worked-solution "The best deal is the one with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three bags. To compute the unit price, we divide the cost by the total number of kilograms.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of kilograms in bag $A$, we get\n\n$$\n7.50÷5=1.50\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $A$ is $$1.50$ per kilogram.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of kilograms in bag $B$, we get\n\n$$\n3.20÷2=1.60\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $B$ is $$1.60$ per kilogram.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of kilograms in bag $C$, we get\n\n$$\n1.70÷1=1.70\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $C$ is $$1.70$ per kilogram.\n\nFrom the above, we see that bag $A$ has the lowest unit price. Therefore, bag $A$ is the best deal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495042 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04d9118c-9cc2-5bb5-a7c4-ce43be06a65d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Best Deal by Computing Unit Prices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40e3f585-edf1-517f-92ac-7bfe4b4a50fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494451} {:answer/id #uuid "5d184d42-ba72-557f-8fb2-70492790ba10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494509} {:answer/id #uuid "779ab6ef-6780-5dd8-828e-b29df0c5b4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494576} {:answer/id #uuid "bd41aa29-8b8e-58da-98d7-399488cd0fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494760} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b94a2e-51bb-5b5f-a4f8-1cdcc321acc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "779ab6ef-6780-5dd8-828e-b29df0c5b4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494576} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd2d7248-3f1b-5a0e-8555-6ff860c97edc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d34abd56-cdca-5fad-b44a-9d3b63e57048" :question/math-academy-id "q-355006" :question/problem "Sarah wonders what bag of coffee to buy from three different types available at the cafe:\n\n- Bag $A$ weighs $12$ ounces, and costs $$9.60\\,.$\n\n- Bag $B$ weighs $16$ ounces, and costs $$12.00\\,.$\n\n- Bag $C$ weighs $8$ ounces, and costs $$6.80\\,.$\n\nWhich bag of coffee is the best deal?" :question/worked-solution "The best deal is the one with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three bags. To compute the unit price, we divide the cost by the total number of ounces.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in bag $A$, we get\n\n$$\n9.60÷12=0.80\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $A$ is $$0.80$ per ounce.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in bag $B$, we get\n\n$$\n12.00÷16=0.75\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $B$ is $$0.75$ per ounce.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in bag $C$, we get\n\n$$\n6.80÷8=0.85\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $C$ is $$0.85$ per ounce.\n\nFrom the above, we see that bag $B$ has the lowest unit price. Therefore, bag $B$ is the best deal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495043 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1321abcb-7c99-5fef-b4fb-975ae5652b90" :knowledge-point/title "Working With a Reciprocal Unit Price"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17bc08cc-ce68-54a0-88b0-250d3b17d066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.1$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494366} {:answer/id #uuid "54cab23f-4a0c-5a15-b2b6-fbddfa6cac4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.01$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494488} {:answer/id #uuid "57c4d878-c44e-5692-852e-cd7b918f8a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.5$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494495} {:answer/id #uuid "899dd541-8347-59be-b1a9-c8801116595a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.25$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494625} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad4b9e6-2690-5992-94d5-668e303ff84f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.2$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17bc08cc-ce68-54a0-88b0-250d3b17d066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.1$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494366} :answer-field/id #uuid "6798386e-aa5b-5822-9c5c-5d9a8e3bdd36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1dc8a6fb-a221-5a8e-ae1b-b509f998884f" :question/math-academy-id "q-355713" :question/problem "At the supply store, the unit price of rope is $$10$ per meter. How many meters of rope can Maya buy for $$1?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the unit rate of $$10$ per meter (dollars per meter). To find how many meters of rope she can buy for  $$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in meters per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal: \n\n$$\n10\\,\\text{dollars per meter}\\,\\to \\,1÷10=0.1\\,\\text{meters per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $0.1$ meters per dollar. Therefore, Maya can buy $0.1$ meters of rope for $1$ dollar."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495046 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d550bb4f-72a6-5b74-b050-8fdc2af6b23f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Total Price from a Unit Price"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65a407fb-2c2e-5eea-9622-e240d63f4b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.45$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494522} {:answer/id #uuid "733f2223-ff58-5735-895f-5c187dcac54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494566} {:answer/id #uuid "79b23ce1-9781-5f36-bbb5-222a6e15ea80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.75$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494580} {:answer/id #uuid "978dbf02-7f59-53c4-bbfa-9a43b780f0e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.60$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494663} {:answer/id #uuid "d013e98b-4bc5-5a90-ba41-2136950f1de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "733f2223-ff58-5735-895f-5c187dcac54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494566} :answer-field/id #uuid "d13f49db-850b-534f-9692-eae74792b2af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c8fda57-c0ae-5471-9e00-bdb61e2342a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-68499" :question/problem "Tom wants to buy some oranges. The price of oranges in a nearby store is $$0.75/lb.$ How much would Tom pay for $3$ pounds of oranges?" :question/worked-solution "The unit price of oranges is $$0.75/lb.$ To find the cost of  $3$ pounds of oranges, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.75\\times 3 & =2.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Tom would pay  $$2.25$ for $3$ pounds of oranges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495042 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04d9118c-9cc2-5bb5-a7c4-ce43be06a65d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Best Deal by Computing Unit Prices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2079d726-3520-5cc5-94e2-91ca16684711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494379} {:answer/id #uuid "78f24c6f-e216-5acb-8e29-463bf8a38116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494579} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e88bdd-cc49-58ec-9a7f-0ad19c2452ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494706} {:answer/id #uuid "b64c3b4a-bb29-586f-840a-70997b94eb6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494743} {:answer/id #uuid "be7604bc-90ba-593e-9e81-d9613f48d7e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b64c3b4a-bb29-586f-840a-70997b94eb6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494743} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce911441-7f5d-51d8-b75e-0b18a93a4e51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a79d949c-d327-5106-907b-7c4645b173b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-68503" :question/problem "At Sandy's shop, there are three different boxes of potato chips:\n\n- Box $A$ contains $15$ ounces, and costs $$3.00\\,.$\n\n- Box $B$ contains $18$ ounces,  and costs $$3.24\\,.$\n\n- Box $C$ contains $24$ ounces, and costs $$4.32\\,.$\n\nWhich box of potato chips is the best deal?" :question/worked-solution "The best deal is the box with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three boxes. To compute the unit price, we divide the price by the total number of ounces.   \n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in box $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n3.00÷15=0.20\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $A$ is $$0.20.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in box $B,$ we get\n\n$$\n3.24÷18=0.18\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $B$ is $$0.18.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in box $C,$ we get\n\n$$\n4.32÷24=0.18\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $C$ is $$0.18$ too.\n\nFrom the above, we see that boxes $B$ and  $C$ have the lowest unit price of $$0.18/\\text{oz}.$ Therefore, boxes $B$ and $C$ offer the best deal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495043 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1321abcb-7c99-5fef-b4fb-975ae5652b90" :knowledge-point/title "Working With a Reciprocal Unit Price"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a80ed70-1bfc-5c97-b651-ba289de9f0ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494369} {:answer/id #uuid "6e573580-6cee-55a0-a9d6-143660f23dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494549} {:answer/id #uuid "abd8eae1-1830-5d87-9c09-fdcfe19969d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494716} {:answer/id #uuid "ad24ea14-6842-5cc2-87ae-52d340f8a824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494718} {:answer/id #uuid "d98608bc-b256-528b-afa5-92f92633b657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e573580-6cee-55a0-a9d6-143660f23dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494549} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd5b6bad-d609-5af7-a23d-e7ae41146ca0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e6dd181-7d62-533c-99d8-31ea5383ac30" :question/math-academy-id "q-68507" :question/problem "Mia charges $$0.20$ per minute for babysitting. For how long did she babysit if she earns $1$ dollar?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the unit rate of $$0.2$ per minute (dollars per minute). To find how many minutes she babysits for  $$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in minutes per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal: \n\n$$\n0.2\\,\\text{dollars per minute}\\,\\to \\,1÷0.2=5\\,\\text{minutes per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $5$ minutes per dollar. Therefore, Mia babysits $5$ minutes for $1$ dollar."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495042 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04d9118c-9cc2-5bb5-a7c4-ce43be06a65d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Best Deal by Computing Unit Prices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e71fa60-c756-5470-8cf7-803ae8c3745a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Brands$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494550} {:answer/id #uuid "8a979450-a206-559b-9ca2-6f2697142e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Brands$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494629} {:answer/id #uuid "d97ffa70-b7cb-5140-b264-2ad19f2c7d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Brand $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494832} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3fb457-320d-5b37-b82f-0ecf2efd41dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Brand $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494884} {:answer/id #uuid "fc3e8545-31b8-59e4-85d0-0edecdd41e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Brand $A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d97ffa70-b7cb-5140-b264-2ad19f2c7d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Brand $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494832} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc7e3b27-802f-5d1f-b82a-44ba7a6e2fd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb84f58e-e39d-5a83-ae84-6b21925b6b81" :question/math-academy-id "q-68895" :question/problem "At Chan's SuperMarket, there are three different bottles of spicy peach BBQ sauce:\n\n- The $17$-ounce $A$-brand bottle costs $$14.96\\,.$\n\n- The $19$-ounce $B$-brand bottle costs $$15.96\\,.$\n\n- The $18$-ounce $C$-brand bottle costs $$15.66\\,.$\n\nWhich brand of spicy peach BBQ sauce is the best deal?" :question/worked-solution "The best deal is the one with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three brands. To compute the unit price, we divide the cost by the total number of ounces.  \n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in the $A$-brand bottle,  we get\n\n$$\n14.96÷17=0.88\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of the $A$-brand bottle is $$0.88.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in the $B$-brand bottle,  we get\n\n$$\n15.96÷19=0.84\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of the $B$-brand bottle is $$0.84.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in the $C$-brand bottle,  we get\n\n$$\n15.66÷18=0.87\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of the $C$-brand bottle is $$0.87.$\n\nFrom the above, we see that brand $B$ has the lowest unit price. Therefore, brand $B$ is the best deal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495042 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04d9118c-9cc2-5bb5-a7c4-ce43be06a65d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Best Deal by Computing Unit Prices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43e0ccc5-4f08-510e-8697-e8982768e8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494455} {:answer/id #uuid "4be9c323-4eec-53ad-ae4b-9e07303bdcb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494471} {:answer/id #uuid "9e1fc3e9-6b7e-5bb9-b995-f286738d3db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494679} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2a5cbf-ee94-53c5-a9d9-e968b01ef75a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494754} {:answer/id #uuid "f1906510-55d6-5ff1-aac1-ff539fe0295c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4be9c323-4eec-53ad-ae4b-9e07303bdcb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494471} :answer-field/id #uuid "291a271a-4e84-53aa-a84e-1fa55fac9dd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1900fcae-3d76-5147-a463-152f960f7a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-68909" :question/problem "Martha wonders what bag of oranges to buy from three different types available at the supermarket:\n\n- Bag $A$ weighs $10$ pounds, and costs $$13.30\\,.$\n\n- Bag $B$ weighs $8$ pounds, and costs $$11.20\\,.$\n\n- Bag $C$ weighs $6$ pounds, and costs $$9.00\\,.$\n\nWhich bag of oranges is the best deal?" :question/worked-solution "The best deal is the one with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three bags. To compute the unit price, we divide the cost by the total number of pounds.  \n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of pounds in bag $A$,  we get\n\n$$\n13.30÷10=1.33\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $A$ is $$1.33$  per pound.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of pounds in bag $B$,  we get\n\n$$\n11.20÷8=1.40\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $B$ is $$1.40$  per pound.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of pounds in bag $C$,  we get\n\n$$\n9.00÷6=1.50\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $C$ is $$1.50$  per pound.\n\nFrom the above, we see that bag $A$ has the lowest unit price. Therefore, bag $A$ is the best deal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495043 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1321abcb-7c99-5fef-b4fb-975ae5652b90" :knowledge-point/title "Working With a Reciprocal Unit Price"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "592de0bc-19b2-5beb-ad20-e19a8e44e2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.7$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494497} {:answer/id #uuid "6d807fcc-ea83-559c-8a44-f0783cbcfabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.6$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494545} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea42e11-a0cf-5e31-8b12-e03a025151ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.4$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494551} {:answer/id #uuid "83d04995-ab82-5357-8740-397f838e0200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.9$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494607} {:answer/id #uuid "94b4c6af-75f2-5fd2-8c5f-0fe9eaace917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.8$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94b4c6af-75f2-5fd2-8c5f-0fe9eaace917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.8$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494659} :answer-field/id #uuid "758a60a4-f764-5094-8cfe-aecafffe9082" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "517daee3-2d5a-5b03-be17-46f0dc74f72d" :question/math-academy-id "q-68939" :question/problem "Sarah, a registered nurse, charges $$1.25$ per minute for her work in a rehabilitation center. How long should she work for $1$ dollar?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the unit rate of $$1.25$ per minute (dollars per minute). To find how many minutes Sarah should work  for  $$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in minutes per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal: \n\n$$\n1.25\\,\\text{dollars per minute}\\,\\to \\,1÷1.25=0.8\\,\\text{minutes per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $0.8$ minutes per dollar. Therefore, Sarah should work  $0.8$ minutes for $1$ dollar."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495043 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1321abcb-7c99-5fef-b4fb-975ae5652b90" :knowledge-point/title "Working With a Reciprocal Unit Price"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26149639-344a-5daf-ab19-c899fbfeba5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.4$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494391} {:answer/id #uuid "68f137ee-f9d5-51f6-863f-80c639fff59c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.08$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494529} {:answer/id #uuid "6d166949-53b6-57c4-b1ee-60eaf349f517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.04$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494542} {:answer/id #uuid "7d484d84-b818-5242-95b2-ad6851eff3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.06$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494589} {:answer/id #uuid "ab75b57d-dd4a-541b-b489-edea4ed691a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.8$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68f137ee-f9d5-51f6-863f-80c639fff59c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.08$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494529} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d75103d-90d7-57fe-8873-8e0f248cd292" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "689d0fea-adfc-579c-9b61-c303964b2aec" :question/math-academy-id "q-68942" :question/problem "Bart and two of his friends pooled money together to buy a bottle of blueberry juice whose unit price is $$12.50$ per gallon. How much juice should Bart get if he only puts in $1$ dollar?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the unit rate of $$12.50$ per gallon (dollars per gallon). To find the amount of blueberry juice that Bart could get for $$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in gallon per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n12.50\\,\\text{dollars per gallon}\\,\\to \\,1÷12.5=0.08\\,\\text{gallons per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $0.08$ gallons per dollar. Therefore, Bart should get only $0.08$ gallons of the blueberry juice."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495046 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d550bb4f-72a6-5b74-b050-8fdc2af6b23f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Total Price from a Unit Price"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d633cf9-4173-5078-b2c7-9dd14eaae49f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.30$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494475} {:answer/id #uuid "a9bf3200-0325-5542-b7a2-fade1f2cbd73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.55$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494709} {:answer/id #uuid "b879ec52-feb5-5016-b1db-1d2f346400df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.40$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494749} {:answer/id #uuid "c2052c91-2426-59c5-ae44-f0f821bc2a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494775} {:answer/id #uuid "fae86507-68e7-50ad-82f1-c9c0f63237ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.35$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2052c91-2426-59c5-ae44-f0f821bc2a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494775} :answer-field/id #uuid "dda18ad2-1aab-58db-8a5d-ef25eccb820b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6365884e-1b89-5166-9bc6-57eac1293079" :question/math-academy-id "q-91908" :question/problem "Lucy wants to make a cake and is going to buy some fruit to decorate. How much would it cost her to buy $\\frac{1}{8}$ of a pound of strawberries?\n\n| Item | Price |\n| --- | --- |\n| Peach | $$1.75/\\text{lb}$ |\n| Strawberries | $$2.00/\\text{lb}$ |" :question/worked-solution "The unit price of strawberries is $$2.00/\\text{lb}.$ To find the cost of  $\\frac{1}{8}$ of a pound of strawberries, we multiply: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\times \\frac{1}{8} & =\\frac{2}{1}\\times \\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 1}{1\\times 8} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =2÷8 \\\\  & =0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it would cost Lucy $$0.25$ to buy $\\frac{1}{8}$ of a pound of strawberries."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495043 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1321abcb-7c99-5fef-b4fb-975ae5652b90" :knowledge-point/title "Working With a Reciprocal Unit Price"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "142654a9-a855-540b-9fa9-55d2c199d63e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.30$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494354} {:answer/id #uuid "209a2c44-f250-58f7-a31c-4136773ee413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.50$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494380} {:answer/id #uuid "a5acbe6d-8cf7-50d4-80c3-5d5ef31cbc64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.25$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494696} {:answer/id #uuid "c793ad00-641c-5add-aae4-9d32c9831a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.45$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494788} {:answer/id #uuid "f450ccdf-fdf8-58ab-b3cb-f5dc2dd18c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.4$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f450ccdf-fdf8-58ab-b3cb-f5dc2dd18c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.4$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494899} :answer-field/id #uuid "37a16aac-5fcd-5385-8c8e-82ce5e79b7c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8ca824bb-dbd7-559a-8d0c-91866baabae8" :question/math-academy-id "q-91919" :question/problem "At the vegetable market, the unit price for bananas is $$2.50$ per kilogram. How many kilograms of bananas can Mary buy with $$1?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the unit rate of $$2.50$ per kilogram (dollars per kilogram). To find the number of kilograms of bananas that Mary can buy for $$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in kilograms per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n2.50\\,\\text{dollars per kilogram}\\,\\to \\,1÷2.50=0.4\\,\\text{kilograms per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $0.4$ kilograms per dollar. Therefore, Mary can buy $0.4$ kilograms of bananas."}]}
